module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*

// What a FUSED PAIR does: the two arms the emitter wrote run back to back with the value between
// them left in a register instead of on the stack.
//
// **WHAT IS BOUGHT IS THE INSTRUCTION FETCH, AND THE FETCH IS NOT CHEAP HERE.** `Buf.at<Ins>` is an
// out-of-line call that retains the code buffer, bounds-checks it twice and copies 56 bytes, and
// `bench/results/2026-09-21-sampled-profile.md`'s sampled profile puts it at **12.4% of all sampled
// time** -- one per instruction
// executed. Two instructions run as one pay it once. The push and the pop of the intermediate value
// go with it, which on `AddStoreSlot` and `LessJumpIfFalse` is a `Buf.push<Value>` and a truncate
// that existed only to move a number four bytes.
//
// **WHICH PAIRS WAS MEASURED AND NOT GUESSED.** `profile.sysl` counts adjacent pairs and triples
// under `--features profile`, and the four are the heaviest sequences over `arith`, `loops`,
// `fib`, `nested`, `calls`, `methods` and `dispatch` that are also broad: `LoadSlot`+`PushInt`
// 82.8M, `LoadSlot`+`LoadSlot` 55.0M, `Add`+`StoreSlot` 40.0M, `Less`+`JumpIfFalse` 31.4M. Re-run
// that table before proposing a fifth; a pair costs an arm in the hottest `match` in the project and
// `code.sysl` carries the numbers.
//
// **EACH ONE IS WRITTEN OUT IN `run_frames.sysl`'s OWN ARM, AND A FUNCTION PER PAIR COST 7.4% OF
// WALL.** The four lived here as functions until `bench/results/2026-09-21-inline-fused.md`, which
// is where the number comes from: `private` in sysl is private to the FILE, so a body called from
// `run_frames.sysl` and written in this one cannot be `private` -- and a name the whole module can
// reach lowers to an LLVM `define` with EXTERNAL linkage, whose body has to be kept whatever the
// inliner does. So `-O2` never gets the single-caller bonus that would bring it under the threshold,
// and every fused pair paid a `bl` and a frame around ten instructions -- the very shape 0.0.122 and
// 0.0.123 removed from `Buf.at` and `Buf.push`. **A fast path that has to be inline therefore goes
// in the file its caller is in, and nowhere else**; what stays here is the cold tail below, which is
// a call on purpose.
//
// **EVERY ONE KEEPS THE INT-INT FAST PATH INLINE AND HANDS EVERYTHING ELSE TO `run_arith.sysl`.**
// There is no second copy of an overflow promotion, a string join, an operator method or a
// diagnostic anywhere near the fused arms: a pair of machine integers is answered in the arm and
// every other pair goes to the very function the unfused instruction called -- `added` for
// `AddStoreSlot` and `is_less` for `LessJumpIfFalse`. That is what makes a fused pair
// answer-for-answer identical to the two it replaces, which `tests_fused.sysl` asserts rather than
// assumes.
//
// **`LoadSlot2` READS BOTH CELLS BEFORE PUSHING EITHER**, because a push may grow the stack and
// replace its storage -- and `vm.stack.at` would then be reading the old block. The unfused pair
// could not have this problem, each of its instructions doing one thing.
//
// **`AddStoreSlot` PUTS A WHOLE SUM STRAIGHT INTO THE CELL AND NEVER PUSHES IT**, which is the whole
// of what that pair is worth beyond the fetch: the unfused form pushed the answer, fetched a
// `StoreSlot`, popped it back off and asked `keepable` about it. **An `Int` can never be
// `undefined`**, so the absence rule has nothing to say about the fast path and the check is not
// skipped so much as answered. **Where the sum is not whole it goes through `stored_in_slot` below,
// AND THAT IS ABOUT A SPAN**: the absence refusal belongs to the `StoreSlot` and has to point where
// the unfused one pointed -- a different character from the `Add` the fused instruction carries --
// so reading that span means fetching the instruction the fusion stepped over, which is the very
// cost the fusion exists to remove. The arm pays for it only on the path that genuinely needs it.
//
// **THE SECOND INSTRUCTION IS STILL IN THE CODE BUFFER AND MUST STAY THERE.** `fuse` in `emit.sysl`
// overwrites the first slot of a pair and leaves the second exactly as the emitter wrote it, so a
// jump that lands on it runs the unfused instruction and gets the unfused answer -- which is why no
// fusion here needs anything proved about jump targets. The loop steps over that slot with an extra
// `pc += 1`; forget it and the tail runs twice.
//
// Nothing here is `private`: it is reached from `run_frames.sysl`, which is its only caller.

// What an unfused `StoreSlot` does with a value that is already in hand, which is the tail
// `AddStoreSlot` takes where its sum is not a whole number.
stored_in_slot(vm: *Machine, base: usize, i: usize, v: Value, at: Span) -> Result[unit, Signal]
    keepable(v, at, "written to a local")?
    vm.stack.set(base + i, v)
    Ok(())
